---
import DemoShell from '../DemoShell.astro';
import Control from './Control.astro';
---

<DemoShell
  id="demo-double-layer"
  code="D2"
  title="Electric double layer — PNP"
  poster="d2-double-layer.svg"
  alt="Potential and ion concentration profiles across a nanometre-scale gap: the potential decays over the Debye length while counter-ions accumulate at the electrode, matching the dashed Gouy-Chapman curves"
  aspect="5/2"
  stack={true}
  caption="Potential (left) and ion concentrations (right) next to a charged electrode, solved by Newton iteration on the full Poisson–Nernst–Planck system. Dashed: the analytic Gouy–Chapman profile. The view covers the first ten Debye lengths, where the structure is."
  presets={[
    { label: '1 M: the layer collapses to 0.30 nm', state: { 'd2-c0': 1 } },
    { label: '1 mM in a 20 nm gap: the layers start to fill it', state: { 'd2-c0': 0.001, 'd2-L': 20, 'd2-full': 1 } },
    { label: 'Reverse the electrode: −200 mV', state: { 'd2-v': -200 } },
  ]}
>
  <fieldset class="panel" slot="panel" disabled>
    <legend>cell · potentiostat</legend>
    <div class="control-cols">
      <Control id="d2-v" label="φ applied / mV" min={-300} max={300} value={100} step={5} />
      <Control id="d2-c0" label="c₀ bulk / M" min={0.001} max={1} value={0.1} scale="log" />
      <Control id="d2-L" label="gap L / nm" min={20} max={200} value={50} step={5} />
      <Control id="d2-T" label="T / K" min={273} max={350} value={298} step={1} />
    </div>
    <div class="switch-row">
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="d2-full" /> show the whole gap</label>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-readouts">
      <span>λ_D <output id="d2-lambda">—</output></span>
      <span>C_GC <output id="d2-cap">—</output></span>
      <span>L / λ_D <output id="d2-ratio">—</output></span>
      <span>Newton <output id="d2-newton">—</output></span>
    </div>
  </fieldset>
  <Fragment slot="notes">
    <p>
      Steady Poisson–Nernst–Planck for a 1:1 electrolyte between a blocking electrode (x = 0) and a
      bulk reservoir (x = L). The three unknowns — potential and both ion concentrations — are
      solved together on about 220 nodes graded towards the electrode, with Scharfetter–Gummel
      fluxes and a fully coupled damped Newton iteration, nondimensionalised before assembly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The dashed curves are the Gouy–Chapman solution, which is exact for a semi-infinite gap. The
      numerics land on it while L ≫ λ<sub>D</sub>; shrink the gap or dilute the electrolyte until
      L/λ<sub>D</sub> falls below about ten and the two separate, because the far wall is no longer
      far.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Water at ε<sub>r</sub> = 78.5, point ions, no Stern layer: at high potential the predicted surface concentration exceeds what finite-sized ions allow.</li>
      <li>Blocking electrode: no faradaic current, so this is an equilibrium profile.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</DemoShell>

<style is:global>
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    #demo-double-layer .demo-stage {
      aspect-ratio: 3/4 !important;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { initWhenVisible } from './runtime';
  initWhenVisible('demo-double-layer', async (shell) => {
    const { init } = await import('./d2-double-layer');
    init(shell);
  });
</script>
